good to have you on SpaceX Daily, Episode 20. It's July 2nd, 2026, and I'm Patrick in Vancouver.

00:00:08.420 --> 00:00:11.700
From Starbase to Orbit, here's your SpaceX rundown.

00:00:12.480 --> 00:00:18.640
The 1000th Merlin 1D engine marks how Falcon reuse has turned repeated flights into the

00:00:18.640 --> 00:00:26.280
main driver of engine reliability gains. SpaceX has now completed acceptance testing on the 1000th

00:00:26.280 --> 00:00:32.940
Merlin 1D built for Falcon first stages. Reusability of those engines has allowed post-flight

00:00:32.940 --> 00:00:39.640
inspections that feed directly into design tweaks for higher reliability. The milestone sits inside

00:00:39.640 --> 00:00:46.220
the ongoing Falcon cadence that last appeared in coverage on July 1. Listeners should watch how the

00:00:46.220 --> 00:00:53.320
same reuse data pipeline later informs Raptor iteration cycles. Reddit users noted the milestone as

00:00:53.320 --> 00:00:59.100
a quiet but steady reliability marker, with comments focused on how post-flight inspections have

00:00:59.100 --> 00:01:06.160
iteratively improved component life without major redesigns. Starship 40 completed a 60-second,

00:01:06.300 --> 00:01:12.840
6-engine static fire at the Massey site. Remember, the show covered the Starship Flight Test campaign

00:01:12.840 --> 00:01:19.560
yesterday, where booster catch and full reuse remain the central goals. The test length matches

00:01:19.560 --> 00:01:26.200
the profile needed to clear the vehicle for flight 13. This firing directly checks the 6-engine

00:01:26.200 --> 00:01:32.900
stack before the next flight attempt. Observers will next look for any pad or vehicle anomalies reported

00:01:32.900 --> 00:01:40.500
after the burn. Observers on X track the static fire video frame-by-frame, confirming all 6 engines

00:01:40.500 --> 00:01:46.060
reached full duration with clean plume behavior and no obvious anomalies at the test stand.

00:01:46.860 --> 00:01:54.020
Echo Star satellite TV and wireless units filed Chapter 11, executing a pre-packaged plan after

00:01:54.020 --> 00:02:01.360
spectrum sales to SpaceX and AT&amp;T. The fileings cleared debt tied to the abandoned 5G network

00:02:01.360 --> 00:02:07.740
and allow early repayment. Spectrum assets transferred to SpaceX add capacity that could support

00:02:07.740 --> 00:02:14.520
Starlink direct to cell expansion. Next steps center on regulatory approval of the asset transfers.

00:02:15.660 --> 00:02:22.300
ULA's final Atlas 5 lifted off at 12.30 a.m. Eastern daylight time from Cape Canaveral

00:02:22.300 --> 00:02:30.640
pad 41 on the LEO Atlas 8 mission. The flight was also the last Atlas 5551 configuration.

00:02:31.400 --> 00:02:36.700
The payload supports Amazon's broadband constellation, a direct competitor to Starlink.

00:02:37.500 --> 00:02:42.720
The launch removes one more legacy vehicle from the manifest while Falcon Cadence continues.

00:02:43.680 --> 00:02:49.820
Space enthusiasts shared side-by-side photos of the final Atlas 5 rollout and the active Falcon

00:02:49.820 --> 00:02:57.020
pads, underscoring the generational shift at the Cape. Blue Origin adopted a hybrid rebuild plan

00:02:57.020 --> 00:03:03.600
for launch complex 36 one month after the new Glenn anomaly. The approach aims to restore

00:03:03.600 --> 00:03:10.200
pad capability faster than a full rebuild. The effort keeps a second US heavy lift option in play

00:03:10.200 --> 00:03:16.940
while SpaceX Starship testing proceeds at Starbase. Recovery milestones will be tracked against the

00:03:16.940 --> 00:03:24.100
end of year target. A lawsuit filed over SpaceX's Texas land acquisition alleges the company's activities

00:03:24.100 --> 00:03:30.680
while harm local wildlife. The complaint centers on habitat claims near Starbase expansion zones.

00:03:31.320 --> 00:03:35.440
Any ruling could affect permitting timelines for additional launch infrastructure.

00:03:36.240 --> 00:03:42.480
Observers await the next court filing for schedule impact. A SpaceX Falcon launch is scheduled to

00:03:42.480 --> 00:03:48.260
cross-skyze visible from Phoenix this evening. The trajectory takes the vehicle southeast from

00:03:48.260 --> 00:03:54.640
Vandenberg, producing a visible plume under clear conditions. This continues the Falcon Cadence

00:03:54.640 --> 00:04:00.860
tracked on July 1, where annual launch rate limits remain the open question. Viewers should check

00:04:00.860 --> 00:04:09.240
local weather and exact T-0 timing for best sighting. Florida today reports on the SpaceX and ULA rocket

00:04:09.240 --> 00:04:15.620
launch schedule in Florida. A possible SpaceX Falcon mission is listed from Cape Canaveral.

00:04:16.080 --> 00:04:22.600
It would fly alongside a ULA flight. The schedule reflects continued high cadence operations

00:04:22.600 --> 00:04:29.620
at the eastern range. Falcon reuse counts keep rising. This keeps the annual ceiling question live

00:04:29.620 --> 00:04:35.480
from yesterday's coverage. Operators will monitor range availability for any same day slips.

00:04:36.340 --> 00:04:41.820
Local Florida spotters posted launch window weather models for the possible Cape Canaveral mission,

00:04:42.480 --> 00:04:48.240
highlighting a narrow evening window before Independence Day restrictions. Worth keeping an eye on,

00:04:48.460 --> 00:04:54.420
the lawsuit against SpaceX's Texas land purchase claims the company's expansion will damage local

00:04:54.420 --> 00:05:01.100
wildlife habitats near Starbase. The complaint seeks to block or modify permits tied to additional

00:05:01.100 --> 00:05:07.400
